          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"A faithful minority of God’s people preserved by grace through judgment, exile, or apostasy, often gathered or restored afterward.\",\"book_summary\":\"The faithful few preserved and gathered from what remains, illustrated as gleanings and as precious metal refined from dross.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The divinely preserved faithful minority within God’s broader people.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The preserved faithful few remaining from a larger body.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book emphasizes gathering and purification through metaphors already compatible with standard remnant theology.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither replaces the consensus referent nor excludes any central consensus case.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings identify the remnant as a faithful minority preserved from a larger people. The book’s gleaning and refining imagery adds illustration rather than changing the identification, conditions, or range of cases.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Ruth 2 does not explicitly identify Ruth or the gleanings as “the remnant,” so that portion is typological imagery; nevertheless, 1 Kings 19:18, Zechariah 13:8–9, and Romans 11:5 directly support the preserved, faithful, and refined minority.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
